mr. speaker corona virus continues to  spread throughout the world the latest  figures show that 17,000 337 people have  sadly died here our hearts and the  hearts of the whole house go out to  their loved ones I know that across the  house we are united in our determination  to fight this virus with everything  we've got and today Mr Speaker I wanted  to update the house on each part of our  battle plan first the resilience of the  NHS I can tell the house that for the  first time we now have over 3,000 spare  critical care beds in the NHS that is  more than three times more than we had  at the start of this crisis it's thanks  to the incredible work of an awful lot  of people that we now have this extra  spare capacity even before we include  the new Nightingale hospitals over the  past two weeks I've been lucky enough to  attend either in person or virtually the  opening of four of these new  nightingales in London and Manchester  Birmingham and Harrogate and there are  several more to come all across the UK  in Belfast in Glasgow and Cardiff Exeter  and Sunderland these incredible efforts  from dedicated staff supported by our  armed forces mean that our NHS has not  at any point been overwhelmed by  coronavirus some said this would be  impossible and today I want to reinforce  the message that non covert NHS services  are open for patients the NHS is there  for you if you need advice and treatment  and I want to address very clearly this  message to those who might be vulnerable  to heart attacks or stroke to parents of  young children to pregnant women and to  people with concerns that they may have  cancer I want to emphasize that people  with non coronavirus symptoms must still  contact their GP if you think you need  medical help please contact your GP  either online or by phone to be assessed  if you need urgent medical  nice use NHS one-one-one online or if  you can't get online call one one one  and of course if it's serious or  life-threatening call 999 if you are  told to go to hospital the place you  need to be is in hospital the NHS is  their field and can provide the very  best care if you need it the second  point of our battle plan is on supply  and working to boost supplies of core  equipment now the full weight of the  government is behind this effort and  again we've brought in the Armed Forces  to help us meet this demand this  includes ventilators both purchasing  extra stock and increasing the  production of new ones  we now have record numbers of  ventilators with 10,700 available for  use for patients it includes medicines  so we can make sure everyone has access  to the supplies and treatments they need  and of course it includes personal  protective equipment - in normal times  the NHS PPE supply chain supplies 233  hospital trusts currently 58,000  separate health and social care settings  are being supplied with PPE so we're  creating that whole new logistics  network from scratch and we have some of  the best minds in in the country working  on this I'm grateful to colleagues from  the NHS public health England the crown  commercial service cabinet office  communities Department MOT the Armed  Forces again devolved administration's  territory offices the business  Department of Treasury the Foreign  Office and the Department of  International Trade because they are all  playing their part and last week I  appointed Lord Dayton who delivered the  Olympics to a new role in driving  forward PPE manufacturing here since the  start of this crisis we have delivered  over a billion items of PPE and we're  constantly working to improve the  delivery system and buying PPE from  around the world we're also working to  make more at home and I'd like to thank  the UK businesses who've generously come  forward with offers to turn their  production lines to part of this  national effort and I'd also like to  thank members from across the house who  put us in contact with businesses in  their constituencies we're actively  engaged with over a thousand companies  who buy from abroad and working with a  hundred and fifty-nine potential UK  manufacturers we have a rigorous system  of verifying the offers that we receive  because not all offers have been  credible and it's important to focus on  the biggest most credible offers first  this work is crucial so we can get our  NHS and care staff the kit they need so  they can do their job safely and with  confidence the third part mr. speaker is  to scale up testing I've set the goal of  a hundred thousand tests a day by the  end of this month and I'm delighted to  say that the expansion of capacity is  ahead of plans even though demand has  thus far been lower than expected we are  therefore ramping up the availability of  this testing and expanding who is  eligible for testing to make it and  making it easier to access the tests the  tests are conducted in NHS hospitals  through our drive-thru centres mobile  units and home deliveries these tests  are then sent to laboratories  we've completed the construction of  three lighthouse labs in Milton Keynes  Glasgow and Cheshire each site - took  just three weeks to complete and begin  testing and as we reach have reached the  peak and as we bring the number of new  cases down so we will introduce contact  tracing at large scale the introduction  of the new NHS Act for contract tracing  is also in development and as we do this  we're working closely with some of the  best digital and technological brains  renowned experts in clinical safety and  digital ethics so we can get all of this  right the more people who sign up for  this new app when it goes lives the  better informed our response will be and  the better we can therefore protect the  NHS fourth mr. speaker we need to make  sure the best possible we make the best  possible use of sign  and research to pursue the vaccines and  the treatments that are essential to  debate their virus once and for all  and here the UK is at the forefront of  the global effort we've put more money  into the global efforts to search for a  vaccine than any other country and  yesterday I announced over 40 million  pounds of funding for the two most  promising UK projects Imperial and  Oxford and the vaccine for this Oxford  project will be trialed in people from  tomorrow and I'm sure the whole house  will agree this is a very promising  development and I'll repeat what I said  yesterday which is that in normal times  reaching this stage would take years and  the combination of this innovative group  of people at the Jenner Institute in  Oxford and the MHRA the regulator who  deserve our special praise because they  have both ensuring that the process is  safe yet is also conducted more rapidly  probably than ever before and they  deserve the support of the whole house  in that work at the same time we'll  invest in manufacturing capability  because if either of these vaccines work  then we must be able to make of them  available for the British people as soon  as humanly possible the fifth measure I  will talk about mr. speaker in the time  available is the one where everyone can  play their part social distancing and I  just want to thank everyone from across  the country for their steadfast  commitment in following the rules  including in this house it is making a  difference we are at the peak but before  we relax any social distancing rules or  make changes to them we have set out the  five tests that have to be met first  that the NHS can continue to cope second  that the operational challenges have  been met third that the daily death rate  falls sustainably and consistently  fourth that the rate of infection is  decreasing and most importantly fifth  that there is no risk of a second peak  finally mr. speaker we're working to  protect the most vulnerable through  shielding this is the sixth part of our  battle plan that  in a huge effort underway across  government to contact and support those  at risk  we've been boosted by the support and  the help of the heroic NHS volunteer  responders who signed up in their droves  within two days of our call to action  and unbelievable three-quarters of a  million people put themselves forward  for this initiative so with the  volunteers and with the support of the  communities Department and the NHS and  local councils who've done amazing work  on this that we are shielding the most  vulnerable mr. speaker these are  unprecedented times for us all and we've  all seen the extraordinary impact of  coronavirus in our constituencies and  across the country  and even though today we are physically  separated this house is at its best when  we are united in our purpose and our  resolve so I will keep working with  members right across the house in this  fight against this invisible killer  because this may be a akin to a war mr.  speaker but it's one where the whole of  humanity is on the same side and I  commend this second to the house
 

